<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="58482" href="schedule/2016/fall/ME/598/58482.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2016" href="schedule/2016.xml">2016</calendarYear><term id="120168" href="schedule/2016/fall.xml">Fall 2016</term><subject id="ME" href="schedule/2016/fall/ME.xml">Mechanical Engineering</subject><course id="598" href="schedule/2016/fall/ME/598.xml">Special Topics</course></parents><sectionNumber>NGG</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Thermochem of Reacting Flows</sectionTitle><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>The fundamental physics of chemically reacting flows are presented in this course, with an emphasis on thermodynamics, chemical kinetics, and transport theory.  The course develops the foundation for research in computational or experimental areas of reacting flow.  Applications in the areas of combustion, atmospheric chemistry, pollution control, materials processing, and energy conversion are presented.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2016-08-22Z</startDate><endDate>2016-12-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>01:00 PM</start><end>02:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>206</roomNumber><buildingName>Transportation Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Glumac" firstName="N">Glumac, N</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>